## Inheritance: base classes and overriding

When several classes share data and behaviour, put the shared part in a **base class** and **derive** the others from it. A derived class gets every member of its base, then adds or replaces what it needs.

```csharp
class Vehicle
{
    public string Plate { get; }

    public Vehicle(string plate)
    {
        Plate = plate;
    }

    public virtual decimal Toll() => 2.50m;
}

class Truck : Vehicle
{
    public int Axles { get; }

    public Truck(string plate, int axles) : base(plate)
    {
        Axles = axles;
    }

    public override decimal Toll() => 2.50m * Axles;
}
```

Use inheritance for an **is-a** relationship: a truck is a vehicle. If you'd say "has a" (a car has an engine), use a field instead. That's **composition**, and it's usually the better default: inheritance ties classes together tightly.

### Constructors and base(...)

Constructors aren't inherited. A derived constructor always runs a base constructor first: `base(plate)`, written after a colon, picks which one and passes its arguments. Leave it out, and C# looks for a base constructor with no parameters. If there isn't one, you get error CS7036.

### virtual, override and base

- `virtual` marks a member that derived classes may replace.
- `override` replaces it, and the override runs **even when the object is held in a base-type variable**: with `Vehicle v = new Truck("T1", 3);`, `v.Toll()` returns 7.50. Picking the method from the object's real type at run time is called **polymorphism**.
- Inside an override, `base.Toll()` calls the base version, so you can build on it instead of copying it:

```csharp
public override decimal Toll() => base.Toll() * Axles;
```

**Gotcha:** write a method with the same name but forget `override`, and you've **hidden** the base method instead of replacing it. C# only warns you (CS0114). Code holding the object as a `Vehicle` still calls the base version, so treat that warning as an error.

### protected

`private` members are invisible even to derived classes. `protected` means "this class and the classes derived from it". A common pattern: `public int Health { get; protected set; }` lets anyone read it but only the family change it.

### abstract and sealed

An `abstract` class exists only to be a base: you can't create one with `new` (CS0144). An `abstract` method has no body, and every non-abstract derived class must override it (CS0534).

```csharp
abstract class Shape
{
    public abstract double Area();

    public override string ToString() => $"Area {Area():F1}";
}
```

`ToString` is itself a virtual method of `object`, the base of every class. That's why overriding it changes what `$"{shape}"` prints.

`sealed` does the opposite of abstract: nothing can derive from a sealed class. Seal classes that weren't designed to be extended. Unsealing later is easy; taking it back once others derive from your class isn't.

### New words

- **Inheritance**: a class getting the members of a base class, and adding or replacing some.
- **Base class / derived class**: the class inherited from, and the class that inherits.
- **Polymorphism**: calling a method through a base-type variable and getting the version for the object's real type.
- **Hiding**: a same-named member without `override`, which base-type code never calls.
- **protected**: visible to the class and the classes derived from it.
- **Abstract class**: a class that can't be created with `new`, only derived from.
- **sealed**: a class nothing can derive from.
- **Composition**: building a class from other objects it holds in fields ("has a").

